## 6. Results

We first compare all methods on real data (Section 6.1), then test the adaptive prior on the synthetic suites
(Section 6.2), examine why the sampler behaved as it did (Section 6.3), and close with ablations (Section 6.4) and
computational cost (Section 6.5).

### 6.1 Real-data benchmark

{{ tref('table11_summary') }} reports mean NRMSE, mean rank and win counts at the three training-set sizes. It shows
the better MAPLE route at each size, selected by mean NRMSE on the datasets in the table. {{ R['128'].n_datasets }}, {{ R['512'].n_datasets }} and {{ R['2048'].n_datasets }} datasets
are large enough for $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128, 512 and 2048 respectively (each split keeps at least 200 test
rows).

{{ table('table11_summary') }}

**Overall ranking.** The Friedman test rejects equal performance of the 15 main methods at every size
($\chi^2$ = {{ num(R['128'].friedman.chi2, 1) }}, {{ num(R['512'].friedman.chi2, 1) }} and
{{ num(R['2048'].friedman.chi2, 1) }}; all {{ pval(R['2048'].friedman.p) }}). {{ fref('fig12_critical_difference') }}
shows the ranks and Nemenyi critical differences recalculated for the displayed subset. In the full pre-registered
comparison at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128, global routing (MAPLE-Global) has
the best mean rank ({{ num(R['128'].friedman.avg_ranks['MAPLE-Global'], 2) }}), followed by MAPLE
({{ num(R['128'].friedman.avg_ranks['MAPLE'], 2) }}) and the stacked classical experts
({{ num(R['128'].friedman.avg_ranks['Stack(experts)'], 2) }}). At 512 and 2048 the stacked experts rank first
({{ num(R['512'].friedman.avg_ranks['Stack(experts)'], 2) }} and {{ num(R['2048'].friedman.avg_ranks['Stack(experts)'], 2) }}),
with MAPLE-Global, MAPLE and tuned CatBoost within one critical difference. The three standalone PFNs rank level with
tuned CatBoost at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128 (MAPLE-Net: {{ num(R['128'].friedman.avg_ranks['MAPLE-Net'], 2) }}; CatBoost-T:
{{ num(R['128'].friedman.avg_ranks['CatBoost-T'], 2) }}) but fall to ranks
{{ num(R['512'].friedman.avg_ranks['MAPLE-Net'], 2) }} and {{ num(R['2048'].friedman.avg_ranks['MAPLE-Net'], 2) }} at the
larger sizes.

{{ figure('fig12_critical_difference', 'Mean ranks and Nemenyi critical differences for the displayed methods at each training-set size. The ranks and critical differences are calculated within this subset. Rank 1 is the lowest NRMSE. Lines span one critical difference ($\\alpha$ = 0.05), and the shaded band covers ranks within one critical difference of the best method.') }}

**Routing versus the PFN alone (RQ5).** Routing the PFN together with the classical experts reduces MAPLE-Net's error
at every size, by a median of {{ reduction(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-Net']) }} at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128
({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-Net']) }}), {{ reduction(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-Net']) }} at 512
({{ stat(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-Net']) }}) and {{ reduction(R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-Net']) }} at 2048
({{ stat(R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-Net']) }}). The margin grows because the standalone PFN gets worse relative to
the other methods as $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ grows (Section 6.5), not because the router improves. The second half of
H5, that MAPLE is no worse than stacking, does not hold at every size. Against the stacked experts, the median
difference is {{ gain(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }} in MAPLE's favour at 128
({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}; {{ sig(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}), whereas
MAPLE has higher median error at 512 ({{ pct(-R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)'].median_diff) }};
{{ stat(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}; {{ sig(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}) and at 2048
({{ pct(-R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)'].median_diff) }};
{{ stat(R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}; {{ sig(R['2048'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(experts)']) }}). Against
stacking that includes MAPLE-Net, MAPLE has higher median error at 512
({{ pct(-R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(MAPLE-Net)'].median_diff) }}; {{ stat(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(MAPLE-Net)']) }};
{{ sig(R['512'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_Stack(MAPLE-Net)']) }}). All of these deficits are below the 1% threshold we set for
practical relevance. Local routing did not improve on global routing: at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128, MAPLE-Global had
{{ pct(-R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-Global'].median_diff) }} lower median error than MAPLE
({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-Global'], flip=True) }}; {{ sig(R['128'].rq['RQ5_F_vs_MAPLE-Global']) }}), and the
difference was not significant at the larger sizes either.

**Does the PFN add information to the experts?** In an exploratory comparison, stacking with MAPLE-Net had lower error
than stacking the experts alone at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128
({{ stat(R['128'].exploratory['StackPFN_vs_StackExperts']) }}), but not at 512
({{ stat(R['512'].exploratory['StackPFN_vs_StackExperts']) }}) or 2048
({{ stat(R['2048'].exploratory['StackPFN_vs_StackExperts']) }}). At the larger sizes the two stackers produced
identical errors on {{ R['512'].exploratory['StackPFN_vs_StackExperts'].ties }} and
{{ R['2048'].exploratory['StackPFN_vs_StackExperts'].ties }} datasets respectively, which is what happens when the
non-negative stacker gives MAPLE-Net zero weight.

**Comparison with tuned gradient boosting (RQ6, exploratory).** At $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 128, MAPLE reduced the
median error of tuned CatBoost by {{ reduction(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }}
({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }}), of tuned XGBoost by {{ reduction(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_XGBoost-T']) }}
({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_XGBoost-T']) }}) and of tuned LightGBM by
{{ reduction(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_LightGBM-T']) }} ({{ stat(R['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_LightGBM-T']) }}). At 512 the
difference to tuned CatBoost is {{ sig(R['512'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }}
({{ gain(R['512'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }} in MAPLE's favour; {{ stat(R['512'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }}),
and at 2048 tuned CatBoost has the lower median error
({{ pct(-R['2048'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T'].median_diff) }};
{{ stat(R['2048'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T'], flip=True) }}; {{ sig(R['2048'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T']) }}).


**Per-dataset results.** {{ fref('fig06_nrmse_datasets') }} shows NRMSE per dataset at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 512.
MAPLE was more accurate than all classical baselines included in the benchmark summary on {{ SM.real['128'].benefited | length }} of
{{ R['128'].n_datasets_full }} datasets at 128, {{ SM.real['512'].benefited | length }} of
{{ R['512'].n_datasets_full }} at 512 and {{ SM.real['2048'].benefited | length }} of {{ R['2048'].n_datasets_full }}
at 2048. The standalone PFN failed on a few datasets: on naval propulsion plant its NRMSE was
{{ num(R['512'].nrmse_per_dataset['MAPLE-Net']['naval_propulsion_plant'], 2) }} at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 512, no better
than predicting the training mean, whereas tuned CatBoost reached
{{ num(R['512'].nrmse_per_dataset['CatBoost-T']['naval_propulsion_plant'], 2) }} and MAPLE
{{ num(R['512'].nrmse_per_dataset['MAPLE']['naval_propulsion_plant'], 2) }}. This is consistent with the router
down-weighting the PFN where its out-of-fold error is large. We did not diagnose the PFN failures further.

{{ figure('fig06_nrmse_datasets', 'Per-dataset NRMSE for MAPLE, CatBoost and ExtraTrees at n_train = 512. Values are averaged over split repeats and, for MAPLE, over three pretraining seeds. Grey segments span the range across the three methods. Lower is better.') }}

### 6.2 Synthetic generalisation and the adaptive prior

{{ tref('table09_generalization') }} and {{ fref('fig11_generalization') }} report mean NRMSE on the ID suite and the
seven OOD suites. The mathematical-prior PFNs have the lowest mean NRMSE on the ID suite (MAPLE-Net: {{ num(T9['MAPLE-Net']['id']) }}; ExtraTrees: {{ num(T9['ExtraTrees']['id']) }}; CatBoost:
{{ num(T9['CatBoost']['id']) }}). Averaged over the OOD suites they are level with ExtraTrees (MAPLE-Net:
{{ num(T9['MAPLE-Net']['OOD mean']) }}; ExtraTrees: {{ num(T9['ExtraTrees']['OOD mean']) }}). ExtraTrees is better on
nested compositions, Voronoi regions and regime mixtures, whereas the PFNs are better on held-out families,
stretched parameters, three-way interactions and heavy-tailed noise.

{{ table('table09_generalization') }}

{{ figure('fig11_generalization', 'Mean NRMSE per synthetic suite (200 tasks each; PFN bars averaged over three pretraining seeds). Error bars: 95% bootstrap confidence intervals over tasks. ID = fresh tasks from the training cells; the seven OOD suites differ from training in structure.', full_width=true) }}

**Adaptive versus uniform prior (RQ1).** The adaptive sampler did not improve on the uniform mathematical prior.
On the ID suite, MAPLE-Net had {{ pct(SY.RQ1_nrmse.id.median_diff) }} lower median NRMSE than MAPLE-Net/Uniform
({{ stat(SY.RQ1_nrmse.id) }}); on the pooled OOD suites it had {{ pct(-SY.RQ1_nrmse.pooled_ood.median_diff) }}
higher median NRMSE ({{ stat(SY.RQ1_nrmse.pooled_ood) }}). None of the seven OOD suites shows a significant
difference after Holm correction, so H1 is not supported. On real data the two priors were also indistinguishable;
the median NRMSE difference of MAPLE-Net relative to MAPLE-Net/Uniform was {% for g in ['128', '512', '2048'] %}{{ pct(-R[g].rq['RQ1_real_D_vs_A'].median_diff, 2, signed=True) }} at {{ g }} ($p_{\mathrm{Holm}}$ = {{ num(R[g].rq['RQ1_real_D_vs_A'].p_holm_family, 3) }}){{ ', ' if loop.index == 1 else (' and ' if not loop.last else '') }}{% endfor %}
(negative = MAPLE-Net lower error).

**Mathematical versus SCM prior (RQ4).** The PFN pretrained on the SCM prior is far less accurate on every synthetic
suite (ID {{ num(T9['MAPLE-Net/SCM']['id']) }}, OOD mean {{ num(T9['MAPLE-Net/SCM']['OOD mean']) }}; MAPLE-Net/Uniform had
{{ pct(SY.RQ4_synthetic_nrmse.ood_A_vs_P0.median_diff, 0) }} lower median NRMSE on the pooled OOD suites,
$p_{\mathrm{Holm}}$ < 0.001). This comparison favours the mathematical prior by construction, because all synthetic
suites come from the same generator. On real data, the stricter test, the two priors are indistinguishable: the
median NRMSE difference of MAPLE-Net relative to MAPLE-Net/SCM was {% for g in ['128', '512', '2048'] %}{{ pct(-R[g].rq['RQ4_D_vs_P0'].median_diff, 1, signed=True) }} at {{ g }} ($p_{\mathrm{Holm}}$ = {{ num(R[g].rq['RQ4_D_vs_P0'].p_holm_family, 3) }}){{ ', ' if loop.index == 1 else (' and ' if not loop.last else '') }}{% endfor %},
and MAPLE-Net/Uniform gave the same picture. H4 is not supported.

### 6.3 Behaviour of the sampler and of the disagreement signal

{{ fref('fig04_adaptation') }} shows how far each adaptive sampler moved away from the uniform prior. The
loss-driven sampler (B) moved far: its final KL divergence from uniform averaged
{{ num(AD.B.kl_final_mean, 2) }} over seeds, and its final family probabilities ranged from
{{ num(AD.B.family_prob_final_min, 3) }} to {{ num(AD.B.family_prob_final_max, 3) }} (uniform:
{{ num(1 / AD.B.n_families, 3) }}). The disagreement-driven samplers (C and D) barely moved: final KL
{{ num(AD.C.kl_final_mean, 3) }} and {{ num(AD.D.kl_final_mean, 3) }}, with family probabilities between
{{ num(AD.D.family_prob_final_min, 3) }} and {{ num(AD.D.family_prob_final_max, 3) }} for D. The reason is the scale of
the scores. Across cells, the gap scores had a standard deviation of {{ num(AD.D.score_sd_final_mean, 3) }}, against
{{ num(AD.B.score_sd_final_mean, 3) }} for the loss scores, and at $\tau$ = {{ S.sampler.tau }} such small differences
produce Gibbs weights close to uniform (Propositions 2 and 5). The sharper temperature $\tau$ = 0.05 moved the
sampler further (final KL {{ num(AD['tau_0.05'].kl_final_mean, 2) }}) without changing accuracy (Section 6.4).
{{ fref('fig05_family_distribution') }} shows the final family distributions: B concentrated on oscillatory
families (trigonometric and damped oscillator), whereas C and D stayed near uniform.

{{ figure('fig04_adaptation', '(a) Family probabilities of MAPLE-Net (seed 0) over the 14 adaptation rounds (cells summed by family). (b) KL divergence of the sampling distribution from uniform for MAPLE-Net/Loss, MAPLE-Net/Gap and MAPLE-Net (mean over three seeds; band: range over seeds).') }}

{{ figure('fig05_family_distribution', 'Final sampling probability of each function family (summed over complexity levels; mean over three seeds) for the loss-driven sampler (MAPLE-Net/Loss) and the disagreement-driven samplers (MAPLE-Net/Gap, MAPLE-Net). Dashed line: uniform.') }}

Re-weighting changed what the model was trained on but not what it learned ({{ fref('fig14_learning_curves') }}).
B's training loss is higher because it over-samples hard cells, yet on held-out probe tasks drawn uniformly from all
cells the error curves of A, B, C and D coincide throughout pretraining.

{{ figure('fig14_learning_curves', '(a) Training loss on each variant\'s own (adaptive) prior (mean over three seeds, smoothed). (b) Excess error on held-out probe tasks drawn uniformly over cells after each adaptation round (mean over seeds).') }}

**Disagreement versus loss as the adaptation signal (RQ2).** On synthetic tasks, MAPLE-Net/Gap had lower error than MAPLE-Net/Loss
overall ({{ gain(SY.RQ2.all) }}; {{ stat(SY.RQ2.all) }}) and on the noisier half of the tasks
({{ gain(SY.RQ2.high_noise) }}; {{ stat(SY.RQ2.high_noise) }}). These effects are statistically
{{ 'significant' if SY.RQ2.all.p_holm_family < 0.05 else 'not significant' }} but
{{ 'practically negligible (below 1%)' if negligible(SY.RQ2.all) and negligible(SY.RQ2.high_noise) else 'above the 1% threshold' }}.
On real data the direction reversed: MAPLE-Net/Loss had lower median error than MAPLE-Net/Gap at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 512
({{ pct(-R['512'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B'].median_diff) }}; {{ stat(R['512'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B'], flip=True) }}) and
2048 ({{ pct(-R['2048'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B'].median_diff) }};
{{ stat(R['2048'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B'], flip=True) }}). The first of these is
{{ 'below' if negligible(R['512'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B']) else 'above' }} our 1% threshold for practical
relevance{{ ' and so is the second' if negligible(R['2048'].rq['RQ2_real_C_vs_B']) else '; the second is just above it' }},
so on real data the loss-driven sampler was, if anything, slightly better.

**Does the gap track the PFN's error (RQ3)?** {{ fref('fig13_disagreement_vs_error') }} plots the label-free gap
against the PFN's excess error. On synthetic tasks the task-level Spearman correlation for MAPLE-Net is
{{ num(SY.RQ3['MAPLE-Net'].spearman, 2) }} (95% CI [{{ num(SY.RQ3['MAPLE-Net'].ci[0], 2) }},
{{ num(SY.RQ3['MAPLE-Net'].ci[1], 2) }}], n = {{ SY.RQ3['MAPLE-Net'].n }}), far below the
{{ num(SY.RQ3['MAPLE-Net'].spearman_loss_vs_excess, 2) }} of the label-based loss. The committee was more accurate than
the PFN on only {{ pct(SY.RQ3['MAPLE-Net'].frac_committee_better, 0) }} of tasks. Proposition 4(b) guarantees that the
gap tracks excess error only in that regime; elsewhere the gap also measures the committee's own error. The
correlation was weakest on three-way interactions ({{ num(SY.RQ3['MAPLE-Net'].by_suite.ood_interact3, 2) }}) and regime
mixtures ({{ num(SY.RQ3['MAPLE-Net'].by_suite.ood_mixed, 2) }}). On real data the noise-free target is unknown, so
excess error cannot be measured directly; instead we use the noise-free proxy of Proposition 4(d), the PFN's test
MSE minus the committee's. With the dataset as unit, the gap's correlation with this proxy is
{{ num(SY.RQ3_real.spearman_regret, 2) }} (95% CI [{{ num(SY.RQ3_real.ci_regret[0], 2) }},
{{ num(SY.RQ3_real.ci_regret[1], 2) }}], n = {{ SY.RQ3_real.n_datasets }}), and the median within-dataset correlation
across splits is {{ num(SY.RQ3_real.median_within_dataset_spearman_regret, 2) }}. Its correlation with the PFN's raw
test MSE is higher ({{ num(SY.RQ3_real.spearman_datasets, 2) }}, 95% CI [{{ num(SY.RQ3_real.ci[0], 2) }},
{{ num(SY.RQ3_real.ci[1], 2) }}]), but raw MSE is confounded by each dataset's noise level, which raises both
quantities. H3 is therefore {{ S.tables.table13_rq_verdicts.verdict.RQ3 | lower }}: the gap tracks the PFN's excess
error on synthetic tasks{% if SY.RQ3_real.ci_regret[0] > 0 %}, and on real data once noise is removed{% else %},
but we find no reliable relation on real data once noise is removed{% endif %}.

{{ figure('fig13_disagreement_vs_error', 'Label-free PFN–committee gap versus the PFN\'s error. (a) Synthetic tasks, MAPLE-Net: excess error relative to the noise-free target (log–log; dashed line: identity). (b) Real-data splits: PFN test MSE on standardised targets against the gap on the test points.') }}

