{{ table('table07_ablation') }}

{{ figure('fig09_ablation', 'One-factor ablations relative to MAPLE-Net (seed 0): median relative NRMSE change on synthetic tasks (left) and on real datasets at n_train = 512 (right). Positive = worse than MAPLE-Net. Error bars: 95% bootstrap confidence intervals.') }}

{%- set AB = S.stats.ablation -%}
{%- set T7 = S.tables.table07_ablation -%}
{%- macro holm(run) -%}{{ ('$p_{\\mathrm{Holm}}$ < 0.001' if T7['p (real)'][run] < 0.001 else '$p_{\\mathrm{Holm}}$ = ' ~ ('%.3f' | format(T7['p (real)'][run]))) }}{%- endmacro -%}
Three factors produce effects well beyond the seed variation (p-values in this subsection are Holm-corrected across
the rows of {{ tref('table07_ablation') }}). The prior family matters most on synthetic tasks: the SCM prior (MAPLE-Net/SCM)
increases NRMSE by {{ pct(AB.P0.synthetic.delta_vs_ref) }}, but its effect on real data
({{ pct(AB.P0.real512.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }}; {{ holm('MAPLE-Net/SCM') }}) is not significant. Pretraining compute also
matters on synthetic tasks: a quarter of the steps costs {{ pct(AB['budget_0.25_D'].synthetic.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }}
and half the steps {{ pct(AB['budget_0.5_D'].synthetic.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }}, and the uniform-prior runs with
the same budget cuts, also compared with MAPLE-Net seed 0, show the same pattern
({{ pct(AB['budget_0.25_A'].synthetic.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }} and
{{ pct(AB['budget_0.5_A'].synthetic.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }}). On real data the budget effects are small
(between {{ pct([AB['budget_0.25_D'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.5_D'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.25_A'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.5_A'].real512.delta_vs_ref] | min, signed=True) }}
and {{ pct([AB['budget_0.25_D'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.5_D'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.25_A'].real512.delta_vs_ref, AB['budget_0.5_A'].real512.delta_vs_ref] | max, signed=True) }})
and significant for {% set ns = namespace(k=0) %}{% for r in ['budget_0.25_D', 'budget_0.5_D', 'budget_0.25_A', 'budget_0.5_A'] %}{% if T7['p (real)'][r] < 0.05 %}{% set ns.k = ns.k + 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{{ ns.k }}
of the four runs. Function complexity matters for transfer: pretraining on complexity level 0 only raised
real-data NRMSE by {{ pct(AB.complexity_0.real512.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }} ({{ holm('complexity_0') }}), and
stopping at level 1 by {{ pct(AB.complexity_1.real512.delta_vs_ref, signed=True) }} ({{ holm('complexity_1') }}).
Every change to the sampler itself (score definition, committee size, temperature, floor, EMA, number of probes and
update period) changes NRMSE by less than 1% on synthetic tasks and on real data, within the range spanned by
pretraining seeds.

{{ tref('table08_sensitivity') }} and {{ fref('fig10_sensitivity') }} show the same runs as one-parameter sweeps.
The curves are flat for all sampler hyperparameters and slope clearly only for the training budget and the maximum
complexity.

