### 6.5 Computational cost and scaling

{{ tref('table06_efficiency_n512') }} lists fit time, prediction time and memory at $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = 512, and
{{ fref('fig08_cost_accuracy') }} shows the time and accuracy of selected methods. In the tuning experiment, CatBoost
averaged {{ num(T6['fit time (s)']['CatBoost-T'], 0) }} s to fit per split on one CPU thread
({{ S.n_trials }} random configurations with 3-fold cross-validation). MAPLE's fit time,
{{ num(T6['fit time (s)']['MAPLE'], 1) }} s, is dominated by the out-of-fold fits of the five experts; the PFN
itself needs {{ num(T6['fit time (s)']['MAPLE-Net'], 2) }} s to preprocess the training split and
{{ num(T6['predict time (s)']['MAPLE-Net'], 2) }} s to predict. The PFN runs on a GPU while the other
methods run on one CPU thread, so these timings are not a like-for-like hardware comparison. Pretraining costs about
{{ num(T6['pretraining (GPU-h, one-off)']['MAPLE-Net'], 1) }} GPU-hours per checkpoint, paid once.

{{ table('table06_efficiency_n512') }}

{{ figure('fig08_cost_accuracy', 'Cost–accuracy trade-off at n_train = 512: mean fit-plus-predict time per split (log scale) against mean NRMSE over datasets. Points closer than a few pixels share one label. PFN inference ran on a GPU. The classical methods used one CPU thread.') }}

{%- set T14 = S.tables.table14_scaling -%}
{%- set NS = T14['MAPLE-Net NRMSE'] | list | sort -%}
{{ fref('fig15_scaling') }} follows three datasets from $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ = {{ NS[0] }} to {{ NS[-1] }} (the sizes
available for all three). With the smallest training sets the PFN is the most accurate method (mean NRMSE
{{ num(T14['MAPLE-Net NRMSE'][NS[0]]) }}, against {{ num(T14['CatBoost NRMSE'][NS[0]]) }} for CatBoost), and its
inference time stays almost flat. Its accuracy, however, stops improving after {{ NS[-2] }} training rows
({{ num(T14['MAPLE-Net NRMSE'][NS[-2]]) }} at {{ NS[-2] }}, {{ num(T14['MAPLE-Net NRMSE'][NS[-1]]) }} at {{ NS[-1] }}),
while every tree ensemble keeps improving (CatBoost: {{ num(T14['CatBoost NRMSE'][NS[-1]]) }} at {{ NS[-1] }}).
Above 1024 training rows the PFN sees random 1024-row contexts averaged over bags, so it cannot use all the data,
and its pretraining contexts were no larger than this. The standalone PFN's scaling is therefore limited by design,
which explains why its rank falls with $n_{\mathrm{train}}$ in {{ fref('fig12_critical_difference') }}.

{{ figure('fig15_scaling', 'Scaling with training-set size on three datasets (California housing, kin8nm, physicochemical protein; three repeats each), for the sizes available for all three. (a) Fit-plus-predict time; (b) mean NRMSE. MAPLE-Net uses GPU inference with 1024-row context bagging above 1024 training rows; the other methods use one CPU thread and default hyperparameters.') }}
